[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A node declares the component it documents; membership is never read off a file path

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-COMPONENT-IS-DECLARED
TITLE: A node declares the component it documents; membership is never read off a file path
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: The vocabulary is ruled and the corpus carries it, or a node must belong to something no word can name.
STATEMENT: >>>
A COMPONENT is what a body of nodes is ABOUT -- a plan, the canon's core
architecture, the whiteboard's architecture, and whatever else this repository
comes to document. A node DECLARES its components in the `COMPONENT` field, a
free-word tag list carried by all seven types. Membership is never computed
from a file path.

A node may name several: a mechanism can belong to the scribe and to the view
at once, which a directory cannot express. The vocabulary is a declared table
in the spec, so adding a component is an edit to the canon rather than to a
script -- the same shape [LINK: NAR-CANON-SYSTEMS] and [LINK: NAR-CANON-TABS]
already have.

**The field is live and empty, and nothing reads it yet.** The grammar carries
it, `sdoc` writes it, and no node declares one. Until a corpus and a vocabulary
exist, [LINK: DEC-SYSTEM-IS-A-SOURCE-SET] REMAINS IN FORCE and the page still
switches by path. This decision is open precisely because the vocabulary is the
question, and the vocabulary is not derivable from the directory layout that
currently stands in for it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
A path is for human navigation. It cannot express a node belonging to two
things, it cannot be queried except by prefix, and it forces a cut that the
filesystem happens to make rather than one the design makes. The operator:
"i dont like partitioning via file path. thats for human navigation. graphs can
get more complex. its also difficult to build any sort of query mechanics based
on file paths."

[LINK: DEC-SYSTEM-TAG-ON-EVERY-ELEMENT] asked almost exactly this and deferred
it, reasoning that a TAGS token would enter the six non-narrative types'
contract hash. That objection is answered rather than overruled: COMPONENT is
its own field and is excluded from the hash in `dev/scripts/sdoc_fp.py`, for
the reason PLACE already is -- re-filing a node does not change what it claims.

The cost this buys, and it is real: today membership CANNOT BE WRONG, only
unhelpful, because a path is a fact about a file. A declaration can be wrong and
still look right. Worse, the renderer short-circuits an empty membership list to
always-shown, so a node that declares nothing becomes un-hideable rather than
invisible -- the failure is silent in the direction that does not alarm anyone.
That is the argument for a checked vocabulary rather than free words alone, and
for landing the field before the corpus rather than after.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Filed 2026-08-31 on the operator's directive, with the mechanism landed and the vocabulary left open. The field's POSITION is the only one-way door here and it is still open: appended last today, but strictdoc validates the order of the fields a node ACTUALLY HAS, so while no node carries a value the position and the name are both free. After a migration writes values, moving it costs a rewrite of every node.
<<<
